      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>The UTC+0 Foundation</h3>
      <p>GMT is a fixed astronomical standard that does not change with the seasons. It remains locked at UTC+0. When you measure a time in GMT, you are referencing the absolute baseline used globally for coordinates and international systems.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>The UK Daylight Savings Swing</h3>
      <p>Daylight Saving Time in the United Kingdom dictates the switch to BST (UTC+1) for the summer. From the last Sunday in March to the last Sunday in October, civil clocks across all four home nations are advanced by exactly one hour.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>The London Connection</h3>
      <p>London is the historical center of Greenwich Mean Time, but it does not observe GMT year-round. Scheduling calls or system routines in London during summer requires adapting to BST, shifting your target clock one hour forward.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

