    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>London & East Coast US Overlap</h3>
      <p>The time gap between BST and US Eastern Daylight Time (EDT) is 5 hours. To organize a meeting between London and New York, the ideal window is <strong>1:00 PM to 5:00 PM BST</strong>, which translates to <strong>8:00 AM to 12:00 PM EDT</strong>. This offers a comfortable 4-hour window where both sides are inside local working hours.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>UK & India Business Connection</h3>
      <p>India Standard Time (IST) runs at UTC+5:30. Since BST is UTC+1, the difference between London and New Delhi is 4.5 hours during summer. The best crossover time is <strong>8:30 AM to 12:30 PM BST</strong>, corresponding to <strong>1:00 PM to 5:00 PM IST</strong>. This ensures calls finish before Indian teams wrap up their evening.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>European Harmonisation</h3>
      <p>For UK teams collaborating with mainland Europe (CET/CEST), the difference is permanently 1 hour. This allows nearly complete alignment throughout the workday. A standup scheduled at 9:00 AM BST is 10:00 AM CEST, which requires virtually no schedule adjustments.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>UK & Australia Overlap Challenge</h3>
      <p>Coordinating between London and Sydney during daylight saving represents a massive 9-hour gap. The only business hour overlap falls at <strong>8:00 AM to 9:00 AM BST</strong>, which matches <strong>5:00 PM to 6:00 PM AEST</strong>. For longer workshops, teams should consider asynchronous task handovers.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
